#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063830 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8edb0a6c-f22f-56cf-9545-2fae93896db2" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Continuity at a Point Using the Definition"}] :question/id #uuid "2b7ec4d6-260d-5fcd-bde2-cb84052868cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-10705" :question/problem "Consider the following function:\n${1 + x,\\begin{vmatrix}x \\le 1 \\\\ 1 - x\\end{vmatrix}x > 1$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $f(x)$?\n\n1. $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$\n2. $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^-} f(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 1^+} f(x)$\n3. $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1} f(x)= f(1)$\n4. $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n1. Does $f(c)$ exist?\n2. Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n3. Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x) = f(c)$?\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Using the expression for the first branch of the function, we obtain that\n$f(1)= 1+1= 2$.\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$. $\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n- Statement II is false. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n$lim_(x → 1^{-})f(x)|= lim_(x → 1^{-})(1 + x); = 1 + 1; = 2; lim_(x → 1^{+})f(x)|= lim_(x → 1^{+})(1 - x); = 1 - 1; = 0$\nAs a result:\n$\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^-} f(x) \\neq \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^+} f(x)$\nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ does not exist. $\\:\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n- Statement III is false since the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ does not exist. $\\:\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n- Statement IV is false. Since not all statements I-III are true, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/314/Source/Images/dfa809445d41802185fca6d9c871b1a0.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063831 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa1ddb40-3494-541c-b23e-b15dd98bf669" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reason for a Discontinuity"}] :question/id #uuid "a9fce66e-f0e3-50a6-a2d9-0407b9d91d5e" :question/math-academy-id "e-10706" :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x=2$?\n${3x + 2,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 2 \\\\ 6,\\end{vmatrix}x = 2; x^{3}, \\mid x > 2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2$.\n\n- $f(2) = 6$, so $f(2)$ exists. ${\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and find that they coincide:\n$lim_(x → 2^{-})f(x)|= lim_(x → 2^{-})(3x + 2); = 3(2) + 2; = 8,; lim_(x → 2^{+})f(x)|= lim_(x → 2^{+})(x^{3}); = 2^{3}; = 8$.\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and $\\lim_\\limits{x\\to 2} f(x)=8.\\, \\, {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$ $\\ \\[1ex]$\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: $\\lim_\\limits{x\\to 2} f(x)= 8$, while $f(2) = 6$. ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2$ because\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to 2} f(x) \\neq f(2)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063832 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70029065-69c3-585d-a6a1-cc9d2485a60a" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Continuity to Evaluate a Limit"}] :question/id #uuid "8f95e034-a0fe-5535-9a25-ce549f29c9d1" :question/math-academy-id "e-10707" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$ and $f(2)=2$, then what is the value of\n$\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x\\to 2} \\sqrt{x^2-2f(x)}}\\,$?" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$. According to the definition of continuity, this means that $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 2} f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2} f(x)=f(2)=2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x\\to 2} \\sqrt{x^2-2f(x)}}=\\sqrt{2^2-2\\cdot 2} = 0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251555 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa1ddb40-3494-541c-b23e-b15dd98bf669" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reason for a Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4828487c-e173-576a-b8ae-c55d54b7cabd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251557} {:answer/id #uuid "949206f3-36c5-5bd7-900f-3f917a863e8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251558} {:answer/id #uuid "52b53ca9-f0e8-5cf7-be5e-0fc56cbb340d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251559} {:answer/id #uuid "a4210bd2-af89-5581-92bb-3d48d0366834" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(-2)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251560} {:answer/id #uuid "463be8d0-99a8-520e-a353-74770ce1aee4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(-2)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52b53ca9-f0e8-5cf7-be5e-0fc56cbb340d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251559} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7b7a241-5fdb-5f5f-9b26-60ba337591a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251556}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aae228c9-7097-5c88-acad-0bde9e65eba0" :question/math-academy-id "q-104130" :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ given below have a discontinuity at $x=-2?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{2}, & x<-2 \\\\ 4, & x=-2 \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+4}, & x>-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- $f(-2)=4,$ so $f(-2)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}(\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)^{2}+4}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+4}{2} \\\\  & =4, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-2)^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{4+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the one-sided limits exist but are not equal. $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-2$ because ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)$ does not exist."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251562 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa1ddb40-3494-541c-b23e-b15dd98bf669" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reason for a Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a067ae2-a66a-54fe-8208-9057f295963b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ is a piecewise function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251564} {:answer/id #uuid "6ef08972-33aa-5845-bc2b-4cdc882f0d73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251565} {:answer/id #uuid "ee63c00c-1971-57b2-8a48-1536c895ecb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251566} {:answer/id #uuid "681f4311-729c-592e-a175-9c145b03dae0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)\\ne f(-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251567} {:answer/id #uuid "a36646ee-7402-56ef-9b63-9227a45b7f1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(-2)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "681f4311-729c-592e-a175-9c145b03dae0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)\\ne f(-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251567} :answer-field/id #uuid "298c38df-1b7a-559b-8f9a-3c1a64c60bf4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251563}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7cf2bc26-58b9-5c11-9e2f-19000ce8d101" :question/math-academy-id "q-104132" :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x=-2?$ \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2}{2}, & x<-2 \\\\ 5, & x=-2 \\\\ \\frac{18}{{x}^{2}+2}, & x>-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- $f(-2)=5,$ so $f(-2)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and find that they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}(\\frac{{x}^{2}+2}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)^{2}+2}{2} \\\\  & =3, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}(\\frac{18}{{x}^{2}+2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{(-2)^{2}+2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=3.\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=3,$ while $f(-2)=5.$ $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-2$ because ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)\\ne f(-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251569 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70029065-69c3-585d-a6a1-cc9d2485a60a" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Continuity to Evaluate a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71b699f2-7405-5059-a7ec-7b5c5c37942f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251571} {:answer/id #uuid "1360e0fe-8eaa-57c6-8106-b5133b25fd0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251572} {:answer/id #uuid "29cab8b8-72ad-5d84-a326-05c28d62cf90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251573} {:answer/id #uuid "3d3e13c8-f7e8-5392-a6c2-96992bb41259" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251574} {:answer/id #uuid "9f021756-67d6-5e55-9b02-c6128655c431" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71b699f2-7405-5059-a7ec-7b5c5c37942f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251571} :answer-field/id #uuid "17786bf9-1ca1-59ee-9cea-4709fc3100b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251570}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bdf3ff45-feae-5253-9319-18b21bd915d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-104162" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=7$ and $f(7)=13,$ then what is ${lim}_{x\\to {7}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-f(x))?$" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=7.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 7}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {7}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to 7}f(x)=f(7)=13.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {7}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-f(x)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({7}^{2}-13) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(36) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({6}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8edb0a6c-f22f-56cf-9545-2fae93896db2" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Continuity at a Point Using the Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2711c71-6149-551a-9acf-33f3f3099dda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061247} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d57359-cff3-50fe-afd5-499a71d46ad9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061248} {:answer/id #uuid "48532c79-2864-5249-a7ae-e10014e73a3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061249} {:answer/id #uuid "4e8c73ab-11df-5711-b3d6-ab8d3f8cc940" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061250} {:answer/id #uuid "6153f19f-38b4-5c2e-8e1f-dfdff6d6619c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48532c79-2864-5249-a7ae-e10014e73a3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061249} :answer-field/id #uuid "079004be-e3b9-5b05-a9d9-8f196ec198da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061246}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d96aa7c-3228-53f8-9efa-38bbf767192d" :question/math-academy-id "q-152832" :question/problem "Consider the following function:\n$f(x) = {2x - 1,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 1 \\\\ 2 - x,\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 1$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $f(x)$?\n\n1. $f(x)$ is defined at $x = 1$\n2. $lim_(x → 1^{-})f(x) = lim_(x → 1^{+})f(x)$\n3. $lim_(x → 1)f(x) = f(1)$\n4. $f(x)$ is continuous at $x = 1$" :question/worked-solution "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Using the expression for the second branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(1)=2-(1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement II is true. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(2x-1) \\\\  & =2(1)-1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(2-x) \\\\  & =2-(1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ exists:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=1\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have that ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=1=f(1).$  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since all statements I-III are true, according to the continuity test, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251576 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8edb0a6c-f22f-56cf-9545-2fae93896db2" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Continuity at a Point Using the Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f97b4f3-95fb-5c86-953f-c94d45f6c58a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251578} {:answer/id #uuid "857c034e-d0fc-5b89-9f87-4dc07af174d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251579} {:answer/id #uuid "50c95dc1-e2c5-58bf-b925-b2ff8e1cb413" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251580} {:answer/id #uuid "73db96e1-0290-52c5-8e94-5d9ae0305525" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251581} {:answer/id #uuid "34f6fbdd-23e1-52b8-b625-2c7487c9e0b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "857c034e-d0fc-5b89-9f87-4dc07af174d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251579} :answer-field/id #uuid "517f5a4a-d694-5d8e-a834-0fda70aee1b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251577}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "86e8b7b9-d7d8-5f9b-a33a-a4700cd37236" :question/math-academy-id "q-152913" :question/problem "Consider the following function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+1, & x<0 \\\\ 0, & x=0 \\\\ {e}^{-x}, & x>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $f(x)?$\n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=0$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)$ exists\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=f(0)$\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$" :question/worked-solution "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Using the expression for the middle branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(0)=0.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=0.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement II is true. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(x+1) \\\\  & =(0)+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}({e}^{-x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{0} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=0$ exists:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=1\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, we have that ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=1\\ne f(0).$  $\\,\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since not all statements I-III are true, the function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251583 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8edb0a6c-f22f-56cf-9545-2fae93896db2" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Continuity at a Point Using the Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71f0083b-da14-55ab-8683-9d1e2f675e01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251585} {:answer/id #uuid "969c0fdb-6009-571c-bc7c-ff6b76f9be86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251586} {:answer/id #uuid "e9575c37-fc5a-56f8-a3de-9f9f695dc702" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251587} {:answer/id #uuid "3880a286-8228-5cfd-b329-e68bf6ce00f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251588} {:answer/id #uuid "f3a76e25-b0bc-540a-ac1b-bf188ebde065" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "969c0fdb-6009-571c-bc7c-ff6b76f9be86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251586} :answer-field/id #uuid "c89d7f1e-f33c-53c7-b62c-1fa840e86298" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251584}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ca3f1027-fa37-5b41-836a-b53bef56e3f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-152925" :question/problem "Consider the following function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3-x}, & x\\le -1 \\\\ x+1, & x>-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $f(x)?$\n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-1$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)$ exists\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)=f(-1)$\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Using the expression for the first branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(-1)=\\sqrt{3-(-1)}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-1.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement II is false. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}(\\sqrt{3-x}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3-(-1)} \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}(x+1) \\\\  & =(-1)+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=-1$ does not exist. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement III is false since the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=-1$ does not exist. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since not all statements I-III are true, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251590 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8edb0a6c-f22f-56cf-9545-2fae93896db2" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Continuity at a Point Using the Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbd22ea0-a827-5c8a-806c-db2f6166d818" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251592} {:answer/id #uuid "edf211b8-ec9b-5205-9c5f-22028b8914e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251593} {:answer/id #uuid "2738eccb-b44f-5b91-82ea-d1ce45f3a82b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251594} {:answer/id #uuid "634b81a7-35cd-52ff-8d08-b5ab88d80490" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251595} {:answer/id #uuid "13b8bdcd-e562-5896-8852-2bea96cc4b7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2738eccb-b44f-5b91-82ea-d1ce45f3a82b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251594} :answer-field/id #uuid "03c42f49-eca4-548e-ba2a-946523e99270" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251591}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9fb59805-3754-53e9-acab-d2034fbd41f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-152926" :question/problem "Consider the following function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x, & x<0 \\\\ 1, & x=0 \\\\ 3x, & x>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $f(x)?$\n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=0$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=f(0)$\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$" :question/worked-solution "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Using the expression for the middle branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(0)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement II is true. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(2x) \\\\  & =2(0) \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(3x) \\\\  & =3(0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=0$ exists:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=0\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, we have that ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=0\\ne f(0).$  $\\,\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since not all statements I-III are true, according to the continuity test, the function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251597 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8edb0a6c-f22f-56cf-9545-2fae93896db2" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Continuity at a Point Using the Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "865f9605-d157-562c-a78e-7e32f332ca7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251599} {:answer/id #uuid "235dbeb3-10ba-5180-b565-3def804f73e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251600} {:answer/id #uuid "1ee093d1-d3c3-5215-8ebb-784938c84622" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251601} {:answer/id #uuid "c356702c-14be-5c6d-88f3-b0c32c676e80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251602} {:answer/id #uuid "a8f643b9-9b4d-5170-af69-54725ce4eda2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c356702c-14be-5c6d-88f3-b0c32c676e80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251602} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee64c490-5138-5fc7-9f6b-9c529d9ee604" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251598}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ee5d233d-2aed-58d4-99ac-cdfc6dfd9b29" :question/math-academy-id "q-152930" :question/problem "Consider the following function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-4-x, & x\\le 2 \\\\ -3x, & x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $f(x)?$\n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=f(2)$\n- $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Using the expression for the first branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(2)=-4-2=-6.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement II is true. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(-4-x) \\\\  & =-4-2 \\\\  & =-6 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(-3x) \\\\  & =-3(2) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ exists:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=-6\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have that ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=-6=f(2).$  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since all statements I-III are true, according to the continuity test, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251604 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70029065-69c3-585d-a6a1-cc9d2485a60a" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Continuity to Evaluate a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4fa1ece-0022-5b08-a869-39bb3931c8ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251606} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf659e0-7f4b-50b2-bebe-fbfa59ff5635" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cf659e0-7f4b-50b2-bebe-fbfa59ff5635" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251607} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba3c26cd-5edd-5484-ad12-b972b769b637" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251605}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "80d01b82-6f37-5dce-b739-67c5e497a5ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-342262" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=7$ and $f(7)=5,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{(f(x)+1)^{2}}{x-f(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=7.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 7}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 7}f(x)=f(7)=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{(f(x)+1)^{2}}{x-f(x)} & =\\frac{(5+1)^{2}}{7-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{2} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70029065-69c3-585d-a6a1-cc9d2485a60a" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Continuity to Evaluate a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdb24f55-d2fc-52ce-83cd-1fd764f90d8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251610} {:answer/id #uuid "402b6471-23df-5086-8129-058acbdfa2ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251611} {:answer/id #uuid "4b09ccb1-9233-5249-bb2d-0fb1eb0b92ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251612} {:answer/id #uuid "42fb2d2a-029f-57bf-89dd-1b74e1bf8acf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251613} {:answer/id #uuid "5c3e2839-1fc1-5a57-82a7-ed54147205dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42fb2d2a-029f-57bf-89dd-1b74e1bf8acf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251613} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c486000-eb22-5fa8-b3b3-9399b553d088" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251609}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "11906a0c-0ff1-503c-9c88-c67ebc9ab98c" :question/math-academy-id "q-37947" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{+}}f(x)=4,$ then what is $f(\\frac{1}{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\nf(\\frac{1}{2})={lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{+}}f(x)=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064669 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70029065-69c3-585d-a6a1-cc9d2485a60a" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Continuity to Evaluate a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "927989fa-3ca9-535f-8667-2d7f8b8dc4c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062526} {:answer/id #uuid "9f41ec2e-7382-50fe-8c29-da9f1349dda7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062527} {:answer/id #uuid "cd12c798-6560-5ce6-ad17-8f7f85453db5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062528} {:answer/id #uuid "df3eb747-84d0-58f8-8318-efa046a4ffe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062529} {:answer/id #uuid "e6f9c91b-bd39-51ad-8524-e48c0d7b4c92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f41ec2e-7382-50fe-8c29-da9f1349dda7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062527} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a7066f7-a989-5894-895f-42ffb2a7e5ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062525}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4de1a86-cf32-5ec4-aebe-3bc6f5b68dec" :question/math-academy-id "q-38450" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x =-2$ and $f(-2) = 2$, then what is the value of\n$lim_(x → (-2))((f(x))^{2} + 4)$?" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=f(-2)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2)}({(f(x))}^{2}+4) & =({2}^{2}+4)=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064723 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa1ddb40-3494-541c-b23e-b15dd98bf669" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reason for a Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80e4863d-dded-580a-aef2-6a3d29d03756" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ is a piecewise function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062850} {:answer/id #uuid "e7782bc4-9cd0-5561-9785-561eefe94631" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$lim_(x → 3^{+})f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062851} {:answer/id #uuid "207754ad-d4b0-54ee-9d18-145671f8cdbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$lim_(x → 3)f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062852} {:answer/id #uuid "271161a3-9817-5e8a-91ce-2a1b6768ddca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(3)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062853} {:answer/id #uuid "683231ca-3f33-5bcc-bafa-de17af8a392d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "lim_(x → 3)f(x) \\ne f(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "683231ca-3f33-5bcc-bafa-de17af8a392d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "lim_(x → 3)f(x) \\ne f(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062854} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7cc255c-086f-5bda-8a24-0fedbbc29dee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062849}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8fd6c9be-8ceb-56ab-a994-cb6b2495af35" :question/math-academy-id "q-48053" :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x = 3$?\n$f(x) = {x^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}x < 3 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}x = 3; 2x + 3, \\mid x > 3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=3.$\n\n- $f(3)=0,$ so $f(3)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and find that they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={3}^{2} \\\\  & =9, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(2x+3) \\\\  & =2(3)+3 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=9.\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=9,$ while $f(3)=0.$ $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=3$ because ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)\\ne f(3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251615 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70029065-69c3-585d-a6a1-cc9d2485a60a" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Continuity to Evaluate a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc5a24d4-00d7-5d51-8581-9396a643b5d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251617} {:answer/id #uuid "6206ba08-ba2a-56c4-b4d2-8010ef75588f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251618} {:answer/id #uuid "2e158cff-ec6e-57ee-93a0-a9eda22c8a98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251619} {:answer/id #uuid "69eae88e-805e-5680-a836-f20ce88d2729" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251620} {:answer/id #uuid "ea4dc0f8-4ccf-5508-b344-cca9031370f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e158cff-ec6e-57ee-93a0-a9eda22c8a98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251619} :answer-field/id #uuid "995acb94-f53b-5b6a-bb76-28f92f916fa9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251616}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c58af93f-518d-52f1-bdb2-b641e6313e1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-71070" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$ and $f(0)=2,$ then what is the value of\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{({(f(x))}^{2}-3)}^{2}\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=f(0)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{({(f(x))}^{2}-3)}^{2} & ={({2}^{2}-3)}^{2}={1}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251622 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa1ddb40-3494-541c-b23e-b15dd98bf669" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reason for a Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9210bb2e-25c9-503d-90b5-a33ac4060c04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251624} {:answer/id #uuid "af7feb0c-b8e0-554c-9dc9-d4ab75167d28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251625} {:answer/id #uuid "cc2c608a-ea05-5386-ab76-4d83fbbf7f0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251626} {:answer/id #uuid "4064667e-f318-5813-8896-441bce75fe93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)\\ne f(-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251627} {:answer/id #uuid "75975b3d-de90-593a-95c7-2354c820919f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(-2)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4064667e-f318-5813-8896-441bce75fe93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)\\ne f(-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251627} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd4f224f-2e33-5d47-83fd-523e2158759b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251623}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bc0af586-1383-50ca-8658-5c40cee85c12" :question/math-academy-id "q-71076" :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ given below have a discontinuity at $x=-2?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-x, & x<-2 \\\\ 6, & x=-2 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+x, & x>-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- $f(-2)=6,$ so $f(-2)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}({x}^{3}-x) \\\\  & =(-2)^{3}-(-2) \\\\  & =-8+2 \\\\  & =-6, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}(-{x}^{2}+x) \\\\  & =-(-2)^{2}+(-2) \\\\  & =-4-2 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=-6$ $✓$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=-6,$ while $f(-2)=6.$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-2$ because ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)\\ne f(-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251629 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa1ddb40-3494-541c-b23e-b15dd98bf669" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reason for a Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30718148-2db1-52d2-a576-e5ee103848aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251631} {:answer/id #uuid "c93d2672-36dd-5a6e-a7be-bf009a506c37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(-1)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251632} {:answer/id #uuid "84c6432b-bb7d-5d0a-a833-52e9e3ed110c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251633} {:answer/id #uuid "be7e0b08-5a90-57d5-816a-90a03a87c742" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251634} {:answer/id #uuid "52f619d7-987c-589b-b9ee-f7b59e15c5a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52f619d7-987c-589b-b9ee-f7b59e15c5a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251635} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa1ee23b-914b-5080-960d-3273749cf149" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251630}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6c5be8c7-1e7e-5512-992b-9c53ef828d54" :question/math-academy-id "q-71079" :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ given below have a discontinuity at $x=-1?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2}, & x<-1 \\\\ 2, & x=-1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}, & x>-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1.$\n\n- $f(-1)=2,$ so $f(-1)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}(\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}+1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1}{2} \\\\  & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-1)^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=1$ $✓$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=1,$ while $f(-1)=2.$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-1$ because ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1).$"}]}
